WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.640
Some have called it the mother of all battles
and some argue that its television coverage

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.550
changed the face of war.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:10.060
It's hard to get everyone to agree when it
comes to the first Gulf War.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:15.700
The media attention for Saddam Hussein dates
back to August 2, 1990, when the ra s ordered

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.360
his troops to invade neighboring Kuwait, a
small emirate of only eighteen thousand square

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:26.660
km, with 4.5 million inhabitants largely living
in the capital Al Kuwait, but very rich in

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:27.890
oil fields.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:34.289
War broke out on the night of January 16-17,
1991, after a series of ultimatums from the

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:38.579
United Nations asking for the withdrawal of
Iraqi forces from Kuwait.

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:43.579
However, a conflict that didn't last long,
6 weeks, just long enough for a coalition

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:49.559
of 34 countries led by the United States to
showcase its overwhelming superiority by deploying

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:54.329
over five hundred thousand troops and forcing
the Iraqi leader to surrender the following

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:55.329
February 28.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:00:59.940
It was an intense few weeks, however, and
the relentless news coverage ended up making

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.319
the first Gulf War a real media event.

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:07.780
In particular, it was always in the spotlight
of CNN, an all news channel established just

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:12.920
over 10 years earlier, which imposed itself
on the eyes of the world by providing continuous

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.390
news coverage of the progress of Operation
Desert Storm, the actual military action against

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.640
Iraq, preceded by Desert Shield, or the massive
deployment of troops to Saudi Arabia to contain

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.860
Iraqi ambitions.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.509
Television coverage that influenced U.S. foreign
policy decisions to the point of affecting

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:34.109
the course of the conflict, according to what
has been called the "CNN effect."

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:35.460
Then we come back to it.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.421
Before the fateful day of the invasion, no
one had been more concerned about the Iraqi

00:01:39.421 --> 00:01:44.289
leader, even though he had been leading the
Bath regime (from the secular, socialist-inspired

00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:45.929
Bath party) since 1979.

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:51.700
No one, in the context of numerous meetings
among top politicians in the Arab region held

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.609
on the eve of the invasion, guessed or wanted
to guess that Iraq would prove to be an important

00:01:56.609 --> 00:02:01.240
player on the international stage and that
its dictator's policies would pose a threat

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.700
to the world order.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.100
Certainly the crisis triggered by the invasion
of Kuwait can be regarded not only as one

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:12.310
of the major international crises of the postwar
period, but also as a unique event.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:17.480
Indeed, it's the first major post-Cold War
crisis to be located outside the dimension

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.230
of East-West confrontation.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:24.180
For the first time, a member of the United
Nations, Kuwait precisely, hadn't just been

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.720
invaded, but totally occupied and annexed
by another, causing hundreds of thousands

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.420
of displaced people, disruption of trade of
two major oil producers, and worldwide economic

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.420
uncertainty.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:39.510
If we analyze the scale of this war in terms
of casualties, we find that it s far less

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:45.840
than that of the major wars after 1945 (think
of Korea with 4.3 million dead, Vietnam with

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.230
over 2 million, the Iran-Iraq war with over
six hundred thousand, or Lebanon with two

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:56.410
hundred fifty thousand), but from the point
of view of mobilization and armaments used,

00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:01.090
this was, after Korea, the largest interstate
conflict since World War II.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:06.050
Militarily, we can identify three distinctive
features of it: the first is the extreme asymmetry

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:10.970
of casualties, facilitated by the rise of
new technologies: tens of thousands of casualties

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.340
on the Iraqi side, a few hundred on the coalition
side.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.120
The second is the peculiar way the conflict
ended: with a sudden call for a halt by George

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.010
Bush senior, at a time when he could have
annihilated Saddam altogether.

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:28.480
The third and maybe most dramatic is the ecological
disaster that came with it, following the

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.570
Iraqi decision to set fire to Kuwait's oil
fields during the retreat.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:41.680
You remember the famous scene from the movie
"Jarhead," don't you?

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:50.080
- INSERT CLIP - Of course it was not the first
time that war and ecological destruction went

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.230
hand in hand but in this case the destructive
action found no military justification and

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.710
was devastating to the healthiness of the
air over a large area of West Asia.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:02.230
But exactly when did the hostilities date
back to?

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:08.960
The fateful August 2, 1990, is only the culmination
of a process rooted in September 1980, when

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.820
war operations between Iraq and Iran began,
resulting in a conflict that cost more than

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:19.160
half a million dead and ended with neither
winners nor losers but a cease-fire requested

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.739
by Tehran on August 8, 1988.

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:26.490
Of this conflict the American public (but
not the government and secret service of course)

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:31.970
knew almost nothing, just as it was barely
aware of the geopolitical rise of Saddam Hussein's

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:32.970
Iraq.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:38.100
A military power, to be exact the fourth largest
in the world by number of armed forces, after

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:44.270
the USSR, China and the United States: 55
divisions, 1 mln trained men, five hundred

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:47.430
thousand aircrafts and five thousand five
hundred tanks.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:52.530
But it was a power with disastrous public
finances, with an accumulated debt of one

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:57.000
hudnred billion dollars in 1988, due to the
government's huge military expenses during

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.020
the 8 years of bloody war.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:04.120
A debt that couldn't be repaired even by attempting
to promote the private sector and domestic

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.120
agriculture.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.220
But Saddam was convinced that the economy
would recover, believing he could count on

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:14.150
the support of oil lords Kuwait, Saudi Arabia
and the United Arab Emirates.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:19.040
In fact, the 3 countries, along with the United
States and Israel, had supported in various

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.810
forms the Iraq-Iran War of the late 1980s
to contain Shiite fundamentalism provoked

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.710
by the Khomeini revolution, and Saddam expected
them to honor their debt of gratitude, for

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:33.940
benefiting from "Iraq's protective shield
against the Iranian threat."

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:38.150
In response there was a rush to drain the
oil fields and the release of large quantities

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:43.850
of oil onto the market, causing its price
to plummet (from $18 to $13 a barrel).

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:48.240
But the initiative taken by Kuwait was the
straw that broke the camel's back.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.710
You should know that since the 1930s Iraq
had repeatedly claimed sovereignty over Kuwait,

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:58.450
on some occasions, denying its legitimacy
as a separate state, on others, questioning

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:02.580
the boundary delimitation between the two
countries, because the way the United Kingdom

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:08.030
had drawn its border back in 1923 prevented
Iraq from controlling a proper outlet to the

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:12.860
Persian Gulf, corresponding to the disputed
Bubiyan and Warbah islands and a piece of

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.610
desert where the Rumaila deposit is located,
crossing the border between the two states

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.450
and always claimed by Iraq.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:23.889
We said, in the aftermath of the cease-fire
demanded by Tehran, Kuwait, contravening the

00:06:23.889 --> 00:06:29.330
extraction quota agreements established at
OPEC (Organization of the Petroleum Exporting

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:32.900
Countries) increased crude oil extraction
from the Rumaila fields.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.950
This episode, known as "oil theft through
Slant drilling," which is a particular extraction

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:43.770
technique using non-vertical drills, brought
more than $2.4 billion into the country's

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:47.950
treasury, corresponding to about three hundred
thousand barrels of oil per day.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:53.020
An action that collapsed Baghdad's revenue,
which was 90 percent dependent on crude oil,

00:06:53.020 --> 00:06:59.560
by about $7 billion a year, while interest
on the debt also amounted to $7 billion.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.889
In fact, none of the Gulf countries came to
Saddam's aid by proposing to negotiate less

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:09.680
onerous forms of debt repayment, despite the
ra s' constant requests to ease the pressure.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:15.120
So the invasion of Kuwait would offer a multi-layered
solution: a distraction from domestic discontent

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.540
over economic mismanagement, the possibility
of acquiring Kuwaiti assets and investments,

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:24.860
and the seizure of the oil fields themselves,
which was equivalent to controlling about

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.120
one-fifth of all world oil.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.490
Saddam's hostility found confirmation on an
important occasion: at the Arab League meeting

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:38.680
held on July 16, 1990, Iraqi Foreign Minister
Tarek Aziz delivered to the League's secretary-general,

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:44.050
Shadli Klibi, a memorandum in the shape of
a declaration of war, accusing Kuwait of stealing

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:49.360
oil and the remaining Gulf countries of plotting
an economic conspiracy against Iraq aimed

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.120
at putting it out of business once and for
all.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:57.010
We can say that the Iraqi dictator, already
supported in the past by the West, was convinced

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:02.290
that his move would have no practical consequences,
apart from the usual ritual protests.

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:07.870
This is confirmed by the July 25, 1990 summons
of U.S. Ambassador April Glaspie.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:13.180
Saddam gave her barely an hour's notice, Glaspie
didn t make it in time to ask the State Department

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:18.199
for instructions, and the dialogue between
the two resolved into a comedy of misunderstandings:

00:08:18.199 --> 00:08:22.449
too absorbed in the problems of the East,
the diplomat implied that the U.S. didn't

00:08:22.449 --> 00:08:24.710
want to intrude on inter-Arab disputes.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:28.729
The Iraqi dictator, convinced that he had
made his intentions clear, believed he had

00:08:28.729 --> 00:08:31.760
obtained a tacit U.S. "green light" to invade
Kuwait.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.749
An enormous mistake that would cost Iraq dearly,
which, from the time of the coalition ground

00:08:36.749 --> 00:08:42.979
attack dated Feb. 24, 1991, lost control of
Kuwait in just four days.

00:08:42.979 --> 00:08:48.660
Very controversial were the U.S. airstrikes
on the night of Feb. 26-27, directed against

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:52.790
Iraqi forces retreating from Al Kuwait to
the city of Basra.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:57.670
The allies initially bombed the head and tail
of the huge column of vehicles traveling on

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:02.490
the so-called "highway of death," so as to
block the convoy and make it a static target

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.160
for subsequent attacks.

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:09.180
Some commentators suggested that the use of
force had been disproportionate, since the

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:14.949
Iraqis had agreed to withdraw, in compliance
with UN Resolution 660, and the column included

00:09:14.949 --> 00:09:18.920
Kuwaiti prisoners, politicians and Palestinian
civilian refugees.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:24.600
In any case, Saddam remained in power, cracking
down hard on Kurdish uprisings in the north

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:31.240
and Shiite uprisings in the south but had
to accept UN Resolution, pledging to destroy

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:36.950
his arsenals of unconventional weapons, pay
reparations for war damage, and abide by two

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:41.980
security zones created by the coalition, for
Kurds and Shiites precisely.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:47.220
The allied victory, though described by almost
all the Western press as challenging but fair,

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:51.879
with Saddam Hussein compared to Adolf Hitler
and his "fearsome" army to the wehrmacht,

00:09:51.879 --> 00:09:54.140
was easy and with very few casualties.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:58.819
The whole thing, as we shall see, was peppered
with a campaign of mystification in the Western

00:09:58.819 --> 00:10:04.079
press to justify the military intervention
and a spectacular live broadcast by journalist

00:10:04.079 --> 00:10:09.579
Peter Arnett from Baghdad's Rashid Hotel describing
the bombing for hours; it was the first time

00:10:09.579 --> 00:10:14.240
it happened on live television, and many,
including the Italian historian Antonio Scurati,

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.959
say that this contributed to the viewer's
lack of empathy for the war, who from then

00:10:18.959 --> 00:10:24.550
on became accustomed to seeing death and bombs
on the screen not as a chilling loss of life

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:26.930
but as an effective distraction against boredom.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:31.879
We said, the allied victory was easy, but
Bush was unable or maybe unwilling to take

00:10:31.879 --> 00:10:36.649
advantage of the internal tensions in Iraqi
society to deal a decisive blow to Saddam.

00:10:36.649 --> 00:10:42.040
It was felt that a tame but not ideologically
fundamentalist enemy could still come in handy

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:47.350
later, against possible unrest caused by peoples
bordering the Iraqis, apparently friendly

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:49.050
but actually hostile.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:53.879
We mustn't forget, then, that a large section
of Islamic public opinion spoke out in favor

00:10:53.879 --> 00:10:58.759
of Saddam himself, seeing the coalition's
presence as unwelcome Western interference

00:10:58.759 --> 00:11:02.820
in Middle Eastern affairs and damage to the
holy sites of Islam.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:08.051
Saddam, after having always been utterly secular
and completely indifferent to religion, knew

00:11:08.051 --> 00:11:12.879
how to sniff the wind of protest and began
to pose as a protector of Islam, attending

00:11:12.879 --> 00:11:15.649
public prayers and waving the rh toric of
faith.

00:11:15.649 --> 00:11:20.589
His regime survived but internal rivalries
(Kurdish independence, Shiite (shiait) discontent)

00:11:20.589 --> 00:11:24.899
were never quelled and came back to the surface
during the second Gulf War.

00:11:24.899 --> 00:11:26.269
But that is another story.

00:11:26.269 --> 00:11:31.110
Now is the time to talk about CNN effect,
but before we get there, let's see how American

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:35.490
and its allies' intervention in a conflict
that was taking place on the other side of

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.000
the world was justified.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:41.740
The Bush administration tried to convince
world opinion that a real threat was imminent

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:45.339
and the Saudis that an American presence in
the Gulf was inevitable.

00:11:45.339 --> 00:11:52.100
On August 15, 1990, it was George Bush Senior
himself who advanced the first rational explanation:

00:11:52.100 --> 00:11:56.939
"Our jobs, our way of life, our freedom and
that of friendly countries around the world

00:11:56.939 --> 00:12:01.660
would suffer if control of the world's great
oil reserves fell into the hands of that one

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:03.480
man, Saddam Hussein."

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:04.480
Reasons that seemed far-fetched.

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:09.899
There was no real threat to "freedom," except,
as historian Theodore Draper put it, "to that

00:12:09.899 --> 00:12:12.259
freedom to drive your car on cheap fuel."

00:12:12.259 --> 00:12:15.170
But interests in the Gulf area didn't stop
at oil.

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:19.220
Take for example the massive chemical arsenal
in the hands of Saddam Hussein.

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:23.679
Historical reconstructions show how Western
aid was instrumental in the development of

00:12:23.679 --> 00:12:28.089
the Iraqi weapons program, mentioning more
than two thousand companies involved in various

00:12:28.089 --> 00:12:30.290
ways in the formation of the Iraqi war industry.

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:31.290
GRAPHIC The list is as follows: 86 German-Federal
companies, 18 American, 18 British, 16 French,

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:32.290
12 Italian, 11 Swiss, 17 Austrian, 8 Belgian,
4 Spanish; and then Argentina (3 companies),

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:33.290
Japan (1 company), Brazil Egypt, Sweden, Holland,
Poland, India, Monaco, Jersey" (Quote taken

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:34.290
from Pierre Salinger, Eric Laurent, Gulf War.

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:35.290
The Secret Dossier, 1991, p.21).

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:36.290
Washington has long denied possessing information
regarding the chemical arsenal in the hands

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:38.190
of the ra s but recent studies prove otherwise.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:45.570
On Aug. 23, 2013, Foreign Policy published
a survey showing, top secret documents in

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:51.170
hand, that the U.S. was aware of the existence
of these weapons as early as 1984 and was

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:55.619
constantly monitoring Saddam Hussein's plans
for a chemical attack on Iran, which would

00:12:55.619 --> 00:13:00.329
have bent Tehran in 1988 through the use of
sarin gas and mustard gas.

00:13:00.329 --> 00:13:05.509
The CIA had information that for decades remained
in the secret archives and about which the

00:13:05.509 --> 00:13:10.579
government was always silent, even strongly
condemning the use of these instruments of

00:13:10.579 --> 00:13:16.260
war, which, moreover, was prohibited by the
Geneva Protocol of 1925, ratified by the United

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:18.300
States in 1975.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:23.279
Prior to that survey, it was known that the
U.S. had provided Iraq with intelligence support

00:13:23.279 --> 00:13:27.999
and that there was only suspicion that the
chemical arsenal was being used in retaliation

00:13:27.999 --> 00:13:28.999
for war.

00:13:28.999 --> 00:13:33.730
More than suspicions, awareness: in fact,
some declassified documents extracted from

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:39.250
the National Archives in College Park, Maryland,
show how senior U.S. officials had been briefed

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.550
about the extent of the nerve gas attacks.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:46.610
As journalist Shane Harris argued, this amounted
to a "tacit U.S. admission of complicity in

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:51.079
one of the most gruesome chemical weapons
attacks ever launched," in open contrast to

00:13:51.079 --> 00:13:53.480
the Geneva Protocol already mentioned.

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:57.680
And what about diplomatic relations between
the United States and Iraq?

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:01.320
Well they were put at risk even before the
outbreak of the war.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:06.879
On February 15, 1990, Voice of America-the
official radio and television service of the

00:14:06.879 --> 00:14:11.879
U.S. government-broadcast a program appealing
to the public for a general mobilization against

00:14:11.879 --> 00:14:14.279
authoritarian regimes around the world.

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:19.559
Iraq was mentioned almost constantly and Saddam
was portrayed as one of the worst expressions

00:14:19.559 --> 00:14:20.559
of tyranny.

00:14:20.559 --> 00:14:24.930
An apology came immediately from Washington,
but the matter was never digested by the Iraqi

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:25.930
leader.

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:28.589
An apology that Glaspie also made to him,
remember?

00:14:28.589 --> 00:14:32.999
The American diplomat, for how he had been
portrayed in the interview conducted by Diane

00:14:32.999 --> 00:14:38.819
Sawyer on ABC News, claiming that that was
the typical journalistic method used to discredit

00:14:38.819 --> 00:14:40.320
any political figure.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:42.299
But how far did the news media go?

00:14:42.299 --> 00:14:47.619
The news outlets succeeded in legitimizing
the U.S. military intervention in the Gulf,

00:14:47.619 --> 00:14:52.089
overnight transforming Saddam Hussein from
a friend of the West to the embodiment of

00:14:52.089 --> 00:14:56.079
Adolf Hitler, endorsing George Bush senior's
line of thinking.

00:14:56.079 --> 00:15:01.939
Think that the New York Times, in an editorial
published on August 9, 1990 stated that "President

00:15:01.939 --> 00:15:06.999
Bush has drawn a line in the sand, committing
U.S forces to face down Saddam Hussein.

00:15:06.999 --> 00:15:11.939
The costs and risks are enormous, but on balance,
he has made the right choice in the right

00:15:11.939 --> 00:15:12.939
way."(New York Times.

00:15:12.939 --> 00:15:13.939
August 9, 1990 edition).

00:15:13.939 --> 00:15:16.439
Saddam had officially become a U.S. problem,
and the proof lies in the fact that in the

00:15:16.439 --> 00:15:22.110
time span between August 2 and November 8,
1990, the Washington Post and the New York

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:26.720
Times began to cover Iraq, Saudi Arabia and
Kuwait constantly.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:31.399
The former published a total of over one thousand
seven hundred articles on the subject (1742)

00:15:31.399 --> 00:15:35.509
and the latter over two thousand four hundred
(2472), with a focus on news events, obviously

00:15:35.509 --> 00:15:40.939
ignoring the real reasons for the conflict
and why U.S.-Iraq relations had worsened in

00:15:40.939 --> 00:15:41.939
recent years.

00:15:41.939 --> 00:15:47.189
In short, an apt comparison that resonated
with the people, "accepted by 6 out of 10

00:15:47.189 --> 00:15:52.540
Americans," as reported in a poll conducted
between August 9 and 10, 1990 for the New

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:53.779
York Times.

00:15:53.779 --> 00:15:58.230
For the television industry, on the other
hand, the Gulf War was more of a story of

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:01.309
American prowess, especially a celebration
of technological power.

00:16:01.309 --> 00:16:07.019
It s estimated that 17 percent of the images
shown were shots of missiles and cutting-edge

00:16:07.019 --> 00:16:08.310
war technology.

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:14.470
Consider that on January 22, 1991, CNN spoke
of the Patriot missiles as if they were human

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:15.470
actors ("Hailed as one of the first heroes
in the Gulf War, [the Patriot missiles] uses

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:16.470
quick thinking to outsmart the enemy" CNN.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:17.470
Evening edition of January 22, 1991).

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:20.680
But it was also a glorification of the courage,
determination, and skill of American soldiers.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:26.019
If with Vietnam, television had brought to
light the atrocities of war, something different

00:16:26.019 --> 00:16:31.119
was happening with the Gulf: a narrative marked
by the opposition between good and evil was

00:16:31.119 --> 00:16:37.379
adopted, packaged within a real show, complete
with custom logo and music to accompany the

00:16:37.379 --> 00:16:38.379
reports.

00:16:38.379 --> 00:16:39.399
A show expertly hosted by CNN.

00:16:39.399 --> 00:16:44.249
The green light for Operation Desert Storm
came when New York was minutes away from 7

00:16:44.249 --> 00:16:48.559
p.m. on Jan. 16, 1991, the news networks'
prime time.

00:16:48.559 --> 00:16:53.559
On the first night of nonstop bombing, Americans
were addicted to the images of war and didn

00:16:53.559 --> 00:16:56.929
t tear themselves away from the live broadcasts
on CNN.

00:16:56.929 --> 00:17:01.689
This was because shortly after the attack
on Baghdad began, all the networks lost their

00:17:01.689 --> 00:17:03.119
video link from Iraq.

00:17:03.119 --> 00:17:07.929
The only broadcaster to maintain it with its
reporters was precisely CNN.

00:17:07.929 --> 00:17:13.689
On the screens were the images of three journalists:
war correspondent Peter Arnett, reporter John

00:17:13.689 --> 00:17:16.240
Holliman, and TV anchorman Bernard Shaw.

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:21.919
In fact, CNN in September 1990 had managed
to obtain permission from Iraqi officials

00:17:21.919 --> 00:17:26.679
to install a "fourwire" satellite phone, a
device that could withstand signal loss.

00:17:26.679 --> 00:17:32.710
On Jan. 17, all crews present in Baghdad,
including CNN's, were asked to leave the Rashid

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:36.830
Hotel -- headquarters of the international
press -- marked as a coalition bombing target

00:17:36.830 --> 00:17:38.169
for the following night.

00:17:38.169 --> 00:17:42.190
Holliman and Shaw decided to leave, while
Peter Arnett remained on site.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:47.190
The Rashid hotel was never bombed, and for
the next two weeks, Arnett was the only U.S.

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:52.019
citizen broadcasting information and interviews
from Iraq, via the fourwire link carried on

00:17:52.019 --> 00:17:57.149
a truck, with a large CNN initials visible
on the canopy, so that coalition planes wouldn't

00:17:57.149 --> 00:18:00.149
mistake the vehicle for a scud missile shipment.

00:18:00.149 --> 00:18:05.179
So by the end of the month CNN became the
only news provider for more than a hundred

00:18:05.179 --> 00:18:08.450
countries, the primary source of information
from the Gulf.

00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:14.090
To ensure that CNN, as an all news channel
could broadcast twentyfour seven news, two

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:19.519
military analysts were hired on a permanent
basis: former Air Force Major General Perry

00:18:19.519 --> 00:18:23.070
McCoy Smith and military strategy expert James
Blackwell.

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:27.730
It was Major McCoy Smith in one of his books
who explained the formula for the American

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:32.740
broadcaster's success: "CNN had the reporters,
the satellite linkups, the engineers, the

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:36.750
producers, and expert commentators in place
or on standby.

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:41.049
It had a leadership team which understood,
before the war broke out, that this was going

00:18:41.049 --> 00:18:43.663
to be a story of giant proportions."(Perry
McCoy Smith, How Cnn Fought the War: A View

00:18:43.663 --> 00:18:44.663
from the Inside, 1991).

00:18:44.663 --> 00:18:48.289
The job of the analysts was to always be at
the broadcaster's beck and call, always ready

00:18:48.289 --> 00:18:53.549
to comment on the images, to explain the strategic
meaning of a certain bombing and the technical

00:18:53.549 --> 00:18:57.779
details about reconnaissance planes and missiles
dropped on a variety of targets.

00:18:57.779 --> 00:19:03.519
A rather simple example explains the power
of the media in influencing public opinion:

00:19:03.519 --> 00:19:09.799
on January 23, 1991, barely a week into the
war, Major Smith during a broadcast recommended

00:19:09.799 --> 00:19:14.290
reading 3 books to better understand the strategies
behind ongoing operations.

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:19.080
All three sold out by the same evening and
publishers rushed with reprinting.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:20.970
One of the three titles - If War Comes, How
Defeat Saddam Hussein by Trevor Dupuy - boomed

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:24.860
in demand and forced the publishers to reprint
six hundred thousand copies.

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:29.580
However, the persuasive power of CNN effect
also affected foreign policy decisions: a

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:35.460
case study is from February 1991 and is related
to Kurdish refugees who had sought shelter

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:38.529
from Iraqi forces in the mountains southeast
of Turkey.

00:19:38.529 --> 00:19:43.720
Media coverage of the Kurdish crisis was intensive,
emphasizing both the precarious conditions

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:48.350
in which the Kurds were living and the demand
that something be done to help them.

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:51.139
The images shocked the feelings of world leaders.

00:19:51.139 --> 00:19:55.409
British Prime Minister John Major decided
to take the issue to the European community

00:19:55.409 --> 00:20:00.179
and convinced first European partners and
then President Bush to create humanitarian

00:20:00.179 --> 00:20:01.669
corridors in Iraq.

00:20:01.669 --> 00:20:07.009
And so began what would become Operation Provide
Comfort, aimed at getting refugees from the

00:20:07.009 --> 00:20:08.309
Gulf War to safety.

00:20:08.309 --> 00:20:12.680
Barely a year after the conflict in the Middle
East and moving elsewhere, the United States

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:17.220
decided, under heavy media pressure, to intervene
in response to the humanitarian emergency

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:19.610
resulting from the Somali civil war.

00:20:19.610 --> 00:20:25.159
But in October 1993 everything changed: images
of a U.S. soldier killed and dragged through

00:20:25.159 --> 00:20:29.910
the streets of Mogadishu quickly went around
the world, prompting President Clinton to

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.970
withdraw troops from the territory.

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:38.169
John Shattuck, assistant secretary of state
between 1993 and 1998, reveals how the media

00:20:38.169 --> 00:20:42.990
first brought the U.S. into Somalia and then
out of the conflict, according to a so-called

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:44.260
push and pull effect: "The media got us into
Somalia and then got us out" (John Shattuck,

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:45.260
From Massacres to Genocide: The Media, Public
Policy and Humanitarian Crises, 1996, p. 174).

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:48.669
But the ability of televison to determine
what rises to the status of an international

00:20:48.669 --> 00:20:55.429
crisis finds express confirmation in a sentence
uttered by Bill Clinton on July 17, 1995,

00:20:55.429 --> 00:21:00.179
in response to the media call for military
intervention in Bosnia: "We have a war by

00:21:00.179 --> 00:21:01.179
CNN.

00:21:01.179 --> 00:21:04.929
Our position is unsustainable, it's killing
the U.S. position of strength in the world"

00:21:04.929 --> 00:21:06.110
(Bob Woodward, The Choice, 1996, p. 261).

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:09.160
Well, we have also come to the end today,
I thank you all for your attention and see

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:10.160
you in the next video.

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:10.161
Bye!

